From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 14:29:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: serve VK_KHR_pipeline_binary

The local protocol series gives the extension's five commands their command
ids and codecs, so the renderer can now dispatch them. Four are plain
forwards; vkCreatePipelineBinariesKHR creates objects and needs the renderer's
object tracking, because the guest names each VkPipelineBinaryKHR by an id it
chose and later destroys it by that id.

The command is unusual in two ways the dispatch has to respect. Its count is
in-out inside VkPipelineBinaryHandlesInfoKHR, and a driver asks how many
binaries a pipeline yields by passing no handle array at all, so a query
carries no object ids and must create nothing. And when the guest does offer
handles, the driver may fill fewer than it was given, so only the entries the
driver wrote become objects; the rest are released and returned as
VK_NULL_HANDLE. VK_INCOMPLETE is a success for this purpose: it means the
driver filled every handle it was offered and has more to give.

VkPipelineBinaryKHR joins the object handle union and the recognized object
types, and the device teardown path destroys any binary the guest left behind.

vkGetPipelineKeyKHR refuses a request whose VkPipelineCreateInfoKHR carries no
chain. The driver reads that chain for the pipeline it is asked to key and
does not check it first, and the specification gives the command no form that
names no pipeline, so an empty request is one no renderer may forward. The
protocol now carries the chain, so this guards against a guest that sends
something the specification forbids rather than against the transport.

vkGetPipelineBinaryDataKHR drops the blob from a reply whose data size grew
across the call. Every other command that returns a blob beside an in-out size
can only shrink it, so the codec measures the reply's blob by the size the
driver reports and reserves room for it from the size the guest offered. This
command breaks that: a buffer too small for the binary is answered with
VK_ERROR_NOT_ENOUGH_SPACE_KHR, the size the caller needs, and no data at all.
Encoding a blob of the grown size then writes past the reply region the guest
reserved, and the guest decodes a blob longer than its own reply stream, which
aborts the process. A reply that carries no blob is what the specification
describes and what the existing codec already reads, so the guest learns the
size it needs and its buffer is left alone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
